As of my knowledge cut-off date in 2023, `EnvFilter` is indeed part of the `tracing-subscriber` crate in Rust. The `tracing-subscriber` crate contains utilities for implementing and configuring `tracing` subscribers, including filtering and layering functionality.

The `EnvFilter` is used to filter tracing data based on directives from the environment variable typically named `RUST_LOG`. It enables you to control the verbosity of logs at runtime and can be configured to enable more verbose logging for specific modules or less verbose for others.

Here's an example of how you might use `EnvFilter`:

```rust
use tracing_subscriber::EnvFilter;

fn main() {
    // Setup a tracer with the environment filter
    let filter = EnvFilter::from_default_env()
        .add_directive("my_crate=info".parse().unwrap());

    tracing_subscriber::fmt()
        .with_env_filter(filter)
        .init();

    // ... rest of your application code
}
```

